# Coach speech (Lithuanian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Lithuanian needs.
# Register: the coach says "tu" to the player (singular imperative: "Pažiūrėk", "Kirsk", "Eik žirgu");
# the game menus say "jūs". The coach is male ("esu tikras", "aš pasiruošęs"). The player's gender is
# unknown: Lithuanian verbs have no gender in any simple tense ("tu laimėjai", "suklydai" are fine), but
# adjectives, participles, half-participles and "pats/pati" do: never use one that agrees with the
# player ("tu pasiruošęs", "esi laimėjęs", "padaręs ėjimą, ..."): finite verbs, impersonal or noun
# phrases instead ("Ar galime pradėti?", "Kai padarai ėjimą, ...").

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: pėstininkas, žirgas, rikis, bokštas, karalius (masculine) and valdovė (feminine, the only one).
# The possessives tavo / mano and the side genitives baltųjų / juodųjų ("White's" / "Black's") never
# change, so every owner pattern is one word + {piece}; only the noun takes the case. Every form below
# is defined for all six pieces and all four owners (your, my, white, black): any of them can be used
# in any coach file, the analysis included.
#
# Forms (middle column: the knight; the feminine valdovė differs only in its noun, pronouns and endings):
#   nom        tavo žirgas  (the subject; "{your}" alone)  "{your} kabo: niekas jo negina."
#   gen        tavo žirgo   (also after a negated verb: "Nekirsk {my:gen}!"; iš / už / prie / ant / be /
#              dėl / nuo / iki / šalia + gen, po + gen "after")  "Už {your:gen} stovi {t1}."
#   dat        tavo žirgui                          "{my:dat} reikia dar dviejų ėjimų."
#   acc        tavo žirgą   (direct object; į / per / pro / prieš / priešais + acc)  "Galiu kirsti {your:acc}."
#   ins        tavo žirgu   (the instrument: "move with"; su + ins)   "Buvo geresnis ėjimas {your:ins}."
#   loc        tavo žirge                           "Visa grėsmė slypi {my:loc}."
#   voc        tavo žirge / mano karaliau (address)  "Ak, {my:voc}, kur tu eini?"
# Reflexive "savo": when the owner of the piece is the subject of the same clause, Lithuanian says
# "savo", never "tavo" / "mano" ("Perkelk tavo žirgą" is wrong). Same words for every owner.
#   refl_nom   savo žirgas  (only with a dative or genitive subject)  "Man labiausiai rūpi {my:refl_nom}."
#   refl_gen   savo žirgo                           "Nepalik {your:refl_gen} be gynybos."
#   refl_dat   savo žirgui                          "Duok {your:refl_dat} daugiau vietos."
#   refl_acc   savo žirgą                           "Perkelk {your:refl_acc} į {sq}." / "Aš atiduodu {my:refl_acc}."
#   refl_ins   savo žirgu                           "Kirsk {my:acc} {your:refl_ins}!"
#   refl_loc   savo žirge                           "Matai {your:refl_loc} tik gynėją."
# Without a possessive (often the most natural choice when the owner is obvious):
#   bare_nom   žirgas                               "Tai {your:bare_nom}. Jis eina raide L."
#   bare_gen   žirgo                                "Nejudink {your:bare_gen}."
#   bare_dat   žirgui                               "{your:bare_dat} reikia vietos."
#   bare_acc   žirgą                                "Pastatyk {your:bare_acc} į {sq}."
#   bare_ins   žirgu                                "Eik {your:bare_ins}."
#   bare_loc   žirge                                "Visa jėga slypi {your:bare_loc}."
#   bare_voc   žirge / karaliau                     "Bėk, {my:bare_voc}!"
# Pronouns referring back to a piece already named (jis / ji); there are no special forms after
# prepositions ("už jo", "su ja"):
#   pron       jis / ji                             "Tai {your}. {your:pron} eina įstrižainėmis."
#   pron_gen   jo / jos (also "its")                "Už {your:pron_gen} stovi {t1}."
#   pron_dat   jam / jai                            "{your:pron_dat} nebėra kur trauktis."
#   pron_acc   jį / ją                              "{my} gali {your:pron_acc} kirsti."
#   pron_ins   juo / ja                             "Eik {your:pron_ins}."
#   pron_loc   jame / joje                          "Pažiūrėk į {my:acc}: {my:pron_loc} slypi grėsmė."
# Relative pronoun "which" (kuris / kuri), its case set by its role in the relative clause:
#   rel        kuris / kuri                         "{reply} kerta {piece:acc}, {piece:rel} neturėjo gynėjo."
#   rel_gen    kurio / kurios                       "{your}, {your:rel_gen} niekas negina, kabo."
#   rel_dat    kuriam / kuriai                      "{your}, {your:rel_dat} trūksta langelių, įstrigo."
#   rel_acc    kurį / kurią                         "Tai {my}, {my:rel_acc} galėjai kirsti."
#   rel_ins    kuriuo / kuria                       "{your2}, {your2:rel_ins} gynei {your:refl_acc}, pasitraukė."
#   rel_loc    kuriame / kurioje                    "{my}, {my:rel_loc} slypi grėsmė, stovi {sq}."
# Agreement endings (nominative singular, masculine / feminine): an adjective or participle about a
# piece agrees with it. Prefer a verb ("{your} kabo", "{your} įstrigo", "{my} puola {your:acc}"); when
# the adjective is needed, write its stem and the ending form, right after the piece placeholder when
# you can (the coach points at the piece on the first placeholder):
#   agr_as_a   as / a   "{your} surišt{your:agr_as_a}." = surištas / surišta (neapgintas, puolamas,
#              įkalintas, vertas, vienas; also "t{your:agr_as_a}" = tas / ta)
#   agr_us_i   us / i   "{your} saug{your:agr_us_i}." = saugus / saugi (aktyvus, pasyvus, stiprus)
#   agr_es_usi ęs / usi "{your} įstrig{your:agr_es_usi} už pėstininkų." = įstrigęs / įstrigusi (likęs)
#   agr_is_i   is / i   "{your}, ginant{your:agr_is_i} {sq}, ..." = ginantis / ginanti (also šis / ši)
#   agr_is_e   is / ė   "{your} vienintel{your:agr_is_e} gina {sq}." = vienintelis / vienintelė (paskutinis)
# In the accusative singular "-as" and "-a" adjectives agree alike ("palieka {piece:acc} neapgintą").
# Two pieces together ("{t1} ir {t2}") take the masculine plural: "abu puolami", "jie".
