# Number forms. A number takes the case (and, for 1-9, the gender) of the noun it counts. "{n:<form>}"
# looks for number.<n>.<form>.spoken first, then the plain word: a form equal to the plain word is not
# listed. Forms:
#   (none)  masculine nominative   du ėjimai, trys taškai
#   gen dat acc ins loc            masculine: dviejų ėjimų, dviem ėjimams, du ėjimus, dviem ėjimais,
#                                  dviejuose ėjimuose; also the form of every gender-free number (0,
#                                  10-20, the tens and hundreds: dešimties klaidų)
#   f f_gen f_dat f_acc f_ins f_loc  feminine, listed for 1-9 only (the other numbers have no gender):
#                                  dvi klaidos, dviejų klaidų, dviem klaidoms, dvi klaidas, dviem
#                                  klaidomis, dviejose klaidose
#   ord ord_gen ord_dat ord_acc ord_ins ord_loc  ordinals, masculine definite (for "ėjimas"): pirmasis,
#                                  pirmojo, pirmajam, pirmąjį, pirmuoju, pirmajame; listed up to 100,
#                                  101-199 compose ("šimtas pirmasis"), no game reaches move 200
#   mv_acc                         a number with "ėjimas" in the accusative, for "matas per {n:mv_acc}"
#                                  in eval.* (a bare {n} there): vieną ėjimą, tris ėjimus, dešimt ėjimų
# Lines say counted things through count.* (below), which pick these forms. Known limit: in 101-999 the
# hundreds stay in the nominative ("šimtas dvidešimt trijų" instead of "šimto dvidešimt trijų"); the coach
# never counts that high in an oblique case.
